{"timestamp":"2026-10-26T02:58:42.294Z","ordinal":371631,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a17625-5bf6-79d0-b8b4-37bc5a602254","author":"/root/plan05_readonly_review","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/plan05_readonly_review\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** — reviewed the read-only diff at base `62e541c9e170a51d02420753f0807c14d65d7074`; SHA-256 `5a3dfd474764cb90f985a4d5c03bdc16271f1aa797d10d9b71d27610e47f2bfd`.\n\nDurability ordering looks sound: each file is fsynced before its containing directory is synced, and the backup metadata and root are synced before the backup result is returned. Progress contains only aggregate file and byte counts, and the upgrade caller displays it only outside JSON mode.\n\nThe progress callback runs only after a whole file finishes: `copyPrivateFileDurably` reads the file in a loop at [upgrade_journal.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/upgrade-backup-progress/cli/src/upgrade_journal.ts:303), then calls `recordCopied` at [upgrade_journal.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/upgrade-backup-progress/cli/src/upgrade_journal.ts:281). A single large file can therefore leave the user without updates for an unbounded time, despite the one-second interval check. The regression uses 32 small files and only verifies the final callback, so it doesn’t catch this case ([upgrade_transaction_recovery.test.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/upgrade-backup-progress/cli/test/upgrade_transaction_recovery.test.ts:287), [upgrade_transaction_recovery.test.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/upgrade-backup-progress/cli/test/upgrade_transaction_recovery.test.ts:305)).\n\nReport byte progress during the copy loop at bounded intervals, and add a regression proving a large single-file copy reports before completion. No tests were run."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17605-0c8f-7f42-8d4b-980a6c519c84","create_time":1792983522.2944067}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":18233}}
